--TEST--
`Core\Cli\Text::plain` neutralizes its own input, so the sink's one raw path agrees with the sink — ADR 0086 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2: `Text::plain` applies § 1's substitution to its argument. That is what
// makes the raw path a constructor rather than a trust assertion — `echo`
// writes a `Cli\Text` through unchanged, and this is the only text a program
// can put in one.
var $raw = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27) . "[31mred";
echo Core\Cli\Text::plain($raw), "\n";

// § 1: the same bytes reaching the sink as a plain `string` are substituted
// there instead, and `Core\Cli::escape` is that same table asked for a value.
// All three have to agree; a launderer that grew apart from its sink is the
// failure `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named` exists to prevent.
echo $raw, "\n";
echo Core\Cli::escape($raw), "\n";
--EXPECT--
␛[31mred
␛[31mred
␛[31mred
